# Defaults for nektos/act, so `act` and `make ci-*` behave the same for
# everyone. See the "Running CI locally" section of the README.

# The medium image carries the toolchain the setup-* actions expect. The
# default micro image does not, and every job fails on it.
-P ubuntu-latest=catthehacker/ubuntu:act-latest

# act reuses the container between steps within a job, which is what makes a
# second run fast. `make ci-clean` drops them.
--reuse

# Bind the working tree instead of copying it in. Steps that shell out to
# `docker run -v "$PWD/...":...` mount through the *host* daemon, so the path
# they hand it has to exist on the host -- which it only does when the workspace
# is bound. On a GitHub runner the job is not containerised and $PWD is already a
# host path, so this is what makes the two environments agree.
#
# The cost is that a run writes into your real working tree rather than a copy.
# Anything a workflow generates is gitignored; `git status` after a run will tell
# you if that stops being true.
--bind


# Skip act's cache service. The setup-* actions try to save a toolchain cache on
# the way out and spend minutes failing to reach it; --reuse already keeps the
# module and npm caches warm inside the job container between runs. GitHub's own
# caching is unaffected -- it is configured in the workflows, not here.
--no-cache-server
